  { q: "What is the difference between SPF, DKIM, and DMARC?", a: "SPF declares which servers are allowed to send mail for your domain, published as a DNS TXT record. DKIM cryptographically signs each message so the receiver can verify it was not altered in transit and really came from your domain. DMARC ties the two together: it tells receivers what to do when SPF or DKIM fails, and where to send aggregate reports. You need all three working in alignment for strong deliverability and spoofing protection. Ollagraph's email-auth check inspects all three in one call." },
